<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Chach</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 751 CE (approx.) · capital Chach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The early medieval principality of the Tashkent oasis, a Sogdian-Turkic borderland whose ruler's execution helped bring about the Battle of Talas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a>, or Shash, was the oasis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chirchiq-river">Chirchiq River</a> where Tashkent now stands. It emerged as a principality in the 6th century, closely tied to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, whose rulers often installed Turkic tuduns or relatives as local lords alongside a Sogdian-speaking population.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a> controlled a fertile irrigated plain at the meeting point of farmland and steppe, trading with Turkic nomads and producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and weapons. Its coins blended Sogdian and Turkic features, and the ruling house bore Turkic titles. Chinese sources call it Shi, the 'stone' country.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 750 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-xianzhi">Gao Xianzhi</a> executed the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a>, whose son sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>; the resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751 ended Chinese influence west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>. Chach then came under Muslim rule, later as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 500 CE – 751 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.65%;width:5.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 751 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sogdian Zoroastrianism, Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sogdian, Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chach, Shash, Shi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=626&amp;focus=principality-of-chach" aria-label="Principality of Chach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6371 705 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Chach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6691 961L6820 913M6691 961L7771 1192M6691 961L6820 913"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6820 913h0M7771 1192h0M6820 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6691 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6691 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chach. <a href="/atlas?year=626&amp;focus=principality-of-chach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

